C-Kermit is Columbia University's, not this project's, and is not covered by
the repository's root LICENSE.  This directory had no statement of that; the
authority is the notice each source file carries, and this file collects them
so that a reader who finds the tree without the tarball can see the terms.

What this is
------------

C-Kermit 4E(072), 24 Jan 89 -- the version string is the first line of
ckcmai.c.  Taken from the tier-2 MWC BBS copy of the distribution; 21 of our
23 files are byte-identical to it.  A newer COHERENT-supporting release exists
in the holdings (5A(188), 23 Nov 92) and is NOT used, because its own Makefile
says the 16-bit COHERENT target "Does not work, version 5A is too large" and
our 4E binary is already too large to stage.

Terms
-----

Nineteen of the twenty-three files carry a copyright notice.  Seventeen of
them are Columbia's, reproduced verbatim from ckcmai.c:

    Author: Frank da Cruz (fdc@cunixc.cc.columbia.edu, FDCCU@CUVMA.BITNET),
    Columbia University Center for Computing Activities.
    First released January 1985.
    Copyright (C) 1985, 1989, Trustees of Columbia University in the City of New
    York.  Permission is granted to any individual or institution to use, copy, or
    redistribute this software so long as it is not sold for profit, provided this
    copyright notice is retained.

The years differ by file (1985 alone on the files unchanged since first
release; 1985, 1989 on those revised for 4E; 1987, 1989 on ckcdeb.h) and
nothing else does.

READ THE TERMS BEFORE SELLING ANYTHING.  This grant is not BSD and not Free:
it permits use, copying and redistribution only "so long as it is not sold for
profit".  This project distributes an image at no charge, which is what the
grant permits.  It also requires that the copyright notice be RETAINED, and
every one of the nineteen notices is present and unedited in the sources here.

Two files are contributed code with their own copyright holder, on the same
terms in the same words:

    ckudia.c    Copyright (C) 1985, Herman Fischer, 16400 Ventura Blvd,
    ckuscr.c    Encino CA 91436.  "Contributed to Columbia University for
                inclusion in C-Kermit."

(ckuusr.c notes at its XXSHE code that Fischer assigned those rights to
Columbia.)

Four files carry no notice of their own, and none is invented for them here:

    ckopct.c    Howie Kaye, Columbia University, 3/11/86 -- the BOO decoder,
                credited in its header, no grant stated.
    cktock.c    F. da Cruz, CUCCA, Aug 87 -- file-renaming helpers for the
    cktoxk.c    author's own working tree, credited, no grant stated.
    ckcsym.h    a symbol-definition header, no header credit at all.

They came in the same distribution as the eighteen and there is no reason to
read them as separately licensed, but that is a reading and not a statement
they carry.

The Kermit PROTOCOL was developed at Columbia University; the sources say so.

See ../zmodem/COPYING for the other file-transfer program in this tree.
